What it does

An assistant that helps run day-to-day operations for a small business: answering questions about customers/suppliers, tracking invoices and payments, drafting communications, and flagging issues (overdue invoices, low stock from a supplier, etc.). Start with fake/sample data (CSV or SQLite) — no real accounting software integration needed.

Feature plan mapped to each dimension

Tools

search_customers(query), get_customer_details(id)
search_suppliers(query), get_supplier_details(id)
list_invoices(status, customer_id), get_invoice(id)
create_invoice(...), mark_invoice_paid(id)
send_reminder_email(customer_id, invoice_id)
get_overdue_invoices()

Subagents

Billing agent — invoices, payments, overdue tracking, reminders
Customer agent — customer lookups, history, communication drafting
Supplier agent — supplier info, orders, purchase tracking

Router

Classify request → billing / customer / supplier / general, and handle multi-domain asks ("email all customers with overdue invoices" touches billing + customer)

RAG

Index past correspondence, contracts, or notes about customers/suppliers so the agent can answer "what did we agree with Supplier X on payment terms?"

Short-term memory

Session state — "mark that one as paid" refers to the invoice just discussed

Long-term memory

Remember business-specific facts/preferences over time: "always CC accounts@ on invoice emails," recurring patterns per customer (late payer, big client, etc.)

Human-in-the-loop

Any money-related or outbound-communication action — creating an invoice, marking paid, sending a reminder/email — pauses for your approval before executing. This is the most natural and important HITL point in this whole project.

Sandboxing

If you let the agent run analysis scripts (e.g. "calculate total revenue by month from this CSV"), run that in an isolated sandbox rather than directly against your live data

Event streaming

Stream tool-call progress to the UI: "Looking up customer... found 3 overdue invoices... drafting reminder..."

Chat UI

Chat interface with a pending-approvals panel (drafts awaiting your sign-off) — this mirrors what a real internal tool would need

Runtime

Start with SQLite for customers/suppliers/invoices → in-memory checkpointing → persisted checkpointing → think about scheduled/background runs (e.g. daily overdue-invoice check)

Guardrails

Never invent numbers — all financial figures must come from tool calls, not the model's imagination
Never send an email or mark something paid without explicit confirmation
PII/financial data scrubbing before logging
Validate tool inputs (e.g. reject an invoice with a negative amount)

Context engineering

Deciding how much invoice/customer history to include per query, summarizing long customer histories, merging RAG results (past emails) + structured DB lookups into one coherent context

Skills

overdue_followup_skill — prompt + get_overdue_invoices + send_reminder_email + tone/template conventions
new_invoice_skill — prompt + create_invoice + validation rules
customer_summary_skill — prompt + RAG over correspondence + get_customer_details
Phased build order
Phase 0 — One agent, one tool: search_customers over a small fake CSV/SQLite table. Terminal chat loop.
Phase 1 — Add short-term memory + simple chat UI (Streamlit).
Phase 2 — Add invoices data + billing tools (list_invoices, get_overdue_invoices).
Phase 3 — Add RAG over sample customer/supplier correspondence.
Phase 4 — Add event streaming to the UI.
Phase 5 — Add router + split into Billing / Customer / Supplier subagents.
Phase 6 — Add long-term memory (business preferences, customer patterns).
Phase 7 — Add human-in-the-loop for invoice creation/payment/email sending — this is the centerpiece feature for this domain.
Phase 8 — Add sandboxing for any analysis/reporting scripts.
Phase 9 — Add guardrails (no invented numbers, confirmation rules, input validation).
Phase 10 — Harden runtime (persisted DB + checkpoints, scheduled overdue-check job, observability).